00:00:26.360 --> 00:00:30.140
GANG CHEN: OK, so
let's get started.

00:00:30.140 --> 00:00:33.690
A lot of stuff happened
in the last lecture.

00:00:33.690 --> 00:00:35.360
So I'll spend a
little bit more time

00:00:35.360 --> 00:00:40.530
to review what we talked,
what are the key points here.

00:00:40.530 --> 00:00:47.540
So what we did in
the last lecture

00:00:47.540 --> 00:00:50.960
is to ask the question,
what are the electron energy

00:00:50.960 --> 00:00:54.420
levels in a periodic potential.

00:00:54.420 --> 00:00:58.430
And that's really--
the periodic means

00:00:58.430 --> 00:01:01.070
we're start thinking
about the crystals.

00:01:01.070 --> 00:01:05.180
And we started with one
dimensional simple pictures

00:01:05.180 --> 00:01:10.280
where I have periodic
arrangement of atoms.

00:01:10.280 --> 00:01:15.320
And so if you recall,
before last lecture,

00:01:15.320 --> 00:01:19.040
what we consider
really confined states

00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:23.390
of atom, of a molecule,
where the wave function

00:01:23.390 --> 00:01:28.100
is spatially just around the
atom, around the molecule.

00:01:28.100 --> 00:01:31.730
And in this periodic
structure, what

00:01:31.730 --> 00:01:36.200
could happen is the wave
function the original belong--

00:01:36.200 --> 00:01:39.500
electron belong to
an atom, now they

00:01:39.500 --> 00:01:43.910
have space to flex their
wave function muscles.

00:01:43.910 --> 00:01:45.990
It goes through
the whole crystal.

00:01:45.990 --> 00:01:50.540
And in that expression,
what we have is

00:01:50.540 --> 00:01:54.320
we use an extended wave
of different wave vectors.

00:01:57.350 --> 00:02:02.940
And each of these wave
vector represent--

00:02:02.940 --> 00:02:04.540
a wave function.

00:02:04.540 --> 00:02:10.710
And if I have N
atoms, and wave vector

00:02:10.710 --> 00:02:15.840
is every 2 pi, k times a every
2 pi, I have it repeating.

00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:19.540
So at the end, I'm
drawing only 2 pi.

00:02:19.540 --> 00:02:22.950
In fact, I'm drawing from
negative pi to plus pi.

00:02:22.950 --> 00:02:28.650
And I'll give you the rationale
today why we draw this way.

00:02:28.650 --> 00:02:31.740
And then, it's
important to recognize

00:02:31.740 --> 00:02:36.195
that between minus pi
over a to plus pi over a,

00:02:36.195 --> 00:02:40.440
we have a total of N points.

00:02:40.440 --> 00:02:47.860
Because I have N atoms in
this periodic direction.

00:02:47.860 --> 00:02:52.540
And each point, of course,
represents a quantum state.

00:02:52.540 --> 00:02:56.800
Except I do not have
the spin included.

00:02:56.800 --> 00:03:01.990
So each-- so if I have an N
points, in fact, I have 2N.

00:03:01.990 --> 00:03:06.490
So it's symmetric from minus
pi over a to plus pi over a.

00:03:06.490 --> 00:03:08.530
So I only draw--
you see, I only draw

00:03:08.530 --> 00:03:11.560
the other, the one half of it.

00:03:11.560 --> 00:03:14.110
And if I count
from here to here,

00:03:14.110 --> 00:03:16.090
there are really two
important states.

00:03:18.610 --> 00:03:24.020
And with that, I say if I
give hydrogen as an example,

00:03:24.020 --> 00:03:28.600
if I could arrange them in one
dimensional periodical fashion,

00:03:28.600 --> 00:03:30.890
then because each
hydrogen has one

00:03:30.890 --> 00:03:35.290
electron and the absolute zero
temperature, the lowest energy

00:03:35.290 --> 00:03:36.340
level fields.

00:03:36.340 --> 00:03:41.620
And you will fill to
the middle of the band.

00:03:41.620 --> 00:03:47.790
And if I have three electrons,
lithium, you'll fill to here.

00:03:47.790 --> 00:03:50.350
It fills to the
middle of the band.

00:03:50.350 --> 00:03:53.350
And in that-- those
are the metals.

00:03:53.350 --> 00:03:57.340
Where are the
nonzero temperature,

00:03:57.340 --> 00:04:01.450
there are some with very little
energy, you can move around.

00:04:01.450 --> 00:04:07.000
Those electrons near the top,
where the uppermost energy

00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:12.175
level that filled with electron,
this is called the Fermi level.

00:04:14.975 --> 00:04:19.000
At absolute T, the
uppermost energy

00:04:19.000 --> 00:04:22.770
level where electrons
are at the Fermi level.

00:04:22.770 --> 00:04:28.590
And if you read the different
books, sometimes people will

00:04:28.590 --> 00:04:30.750
call nonzero
temperature, they'll

00:04:30.750 --> 00:04:32.790
say that's also Fermi level.

00:04:32.790 --> 00:04:39.840
That is a more better way
is at nonzero temperature,

00:04:39.840 --> 00:04:43.200
this one can shift a little
bit around that position.

00:04:43.200 --> 00:04:48.200
And that's-- it's better to
call them chemical potential.

00:04:48.200 --> 00:04:51.310
So different books,
sometimes they distinguish,

00:04:51.310 --> 00:04:53.090
sometimes they do not.

00:04:53.090 --> 00:04:56.450
A student came to
me and say what

00:04:56.450 --> 00:04:58.880
is say-- in electrical
engineering,

00:04:58.880 --> 00:05:01.520
for example, if you
read electronics,

00:05:01.520 --> 00:05:05.570
they'll always call
that Fermi level.

00:05:05.570 --> 00:05:09.940
Nonzero temperature is
always called Fermi level.

00:05:09.940 --> 00:05:13.960
And so this is, of course,
it fills to the top.

00:05:13.960 --> 00:05:17.710
Then there is a gap in energy.

00:05:17.710 --> 00:05:22.510
That means no electrons can
exist with that specific energy

00:05:22.510 --> 00:05:24.600
in the crystal.

00:05:24.600 --> 00:05:26.930
So no waves allowed.

00:05:26.930 --> 00:05:33.200
And so if I have two
electrons, again, at zero,

00:05:33.200 --> 00:05:40.660
you will fill to the uppermost
of the lowest band there.

00:05:40.660 --> 00:05:44.180
And now, because there
is a gap, electron

00:05:44.180 --> 00:05:47.390
does not have enough energy
to go to the next level

00:05:47.390 --> 00:05:50.100
if that gap is large.

00:05:50.100 --> 00:05:53.990
So for a large gap, the
electron cannot move,

00:05:53.990 --> 00:05:57.800
because there is
no quantum state.

00:05:57.800 --> 00:05:59.670
Now, everything is filled here.

00:05:59.670 --> 00:06:03.350
There's no more quantum state
this electron can move to.

00:06:03.350 --> 00:06:06.110
So the Fermi exclusion
principle say

00:06:06.110 --> 00:06:10.960
you can't have more than one
electron per quantum state.

00:06:10.960 --> 00:06:15.810
So this is the case where
the electron in the material,

00:06:15.810 --> 00:06:18.000
they're always a charge.

00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:19.860
But you got the insulator.

00:06:19.860 --> 00:06:21.760
There is low
electric conduction.

00:06:21.760 --> 00:06:24.860
So this is the insulator.

00:06:24.860 --> 00:06:29.810
And of course, we
spend most of time

00:06:29.810 --> 00:06:35.720
on this one dimensional
lattice chair model.

00:06:35.720 --> 00:06:39.500
And if you go to 3D, which
we'll talk more today,

00:06:39.500 --> 00:06:42.890
you look at each
direction of the crystal,

00:06:42.890 --> 00:06:45.590
you can see a
different periodicity.

00:06:45.590 --> 00:06:48.770
And different periodicity
also means the bonding

00:06:48.770 --> 00:06:51.080
between the atoms in
that specific direction,

00:06:51.080 --> 00:06:54.220
the force is different.

00:06:54.220 --> 00:06:59.500
And so you will see a
different band structure.

00:06:59.500 --> 00:07:02.560
I'm just-- handwavingly
drawing this.

00:07:02.560 --> 00:07:05.920
So this is A1 is one
specific direction

00:07:05.920 --> 00:07:08.950
with that periodicity, A1.

00:07:08.950 --> 00:07:13.310
And another crystallographic
direction could be A2.

00:07:13.310 --> 00:07:16.460
And in fact, there are many
crystallographic directions

00:07:16.460 --> 00:07:17.760
in the crystal.

00:07:17.760 --> 00:07:20.330
So ideally, you know everything.

00:07:20.330 --> 00:07:25.430
You know each direction, what
are the energy diagram, energy

00:07:25.430 --> 00:07:27.180
of the charge.

00:07:27.180 --> 00:07:30.140
And here, I'm drawing
two direction.

00:07:30.140 --> 00:07:35.540
And now, this is the
minimum point here.

00:07:35.540 --> 00:07:41.610
This is the maximum point
in the first band here.

00:07:41.610 --> 00:07:45.590
And again, the band
gap, this is a gap here.

00:07:45.590 --> 00:07:48.320
By the way, when I talk
about the semiconductor,

00:07:48.320 --> 00:07:53.940
I'm talking about the
absolute gap, meaning there--

00:07:53.940 --> 00:07:57.930
this is the highest point
where the electron fills

00:07:57.930 --> 00:08:00.180
and lowest point that's empty.

00:08:00.180 --> 00:08:03.280
And there is a
separation in energy.

00:08:03.280 --> 00:08:06.390
So in this region, there's no--

00:08:06.390 --> 00:08:10.230
in this region here, there's
no electron in the material,

00:08:10.230 --> 00:08:12.830
in the crystal that has--

00:08:12.830 --> 00:08:13.830
could have those energy.

00:08:13.830 --> 00:08:17.350
That's quantum
mechanically forbidden.

00:08:17.350 --> 00:08:21.220
That's what a real
insulator like

00:08:21.220 --> 00:08:26.560
a glass, or quartz, if you
think about crystalline form

00:08:26.560 --> 00:08:28.540
of glass.

00:08:28.540 --> 00:08:30.570
This is where
electric insulator,

00:08:30.570 --> 00:08:33.929
it has a very large bandgap.

00:08:33.929 --> 00:08:37.429
And if this bandgap is small--

00:08:37.429 --> 00:08:39.409
is not that large.

00:08:39.409 --> 00:08:44.300
Let's say silicon is
1.1 electron volt,

00:08:44.300 --> 00:08:47.300
it becomes a semiconductor.

00:08:47.300 --> 00:08:52.520
And for semiconductors, we
have different scenarios,

00:08:52.520 --> 00:09:00.700
where this lowest energy point,
maximum energy point, they

00:09:00.700 --> 00:09:04.870
do not align to the
same wave vector.

00:09:04.870 --> 00:09:07.140
That's the case for silicon.

00:09:07.140 --> 00:09:10.170
And that's an indirect
gap semiconductor.

00:09:10.170 --> 00:09:13.440
So we have different
type of semiconductors.

00:09:16.090 --> 00:09:18.880
We have direct gap.

00:09:24.820 --> 00:09:26.560
We have indirect gap.

00:09:35.120 --> 00:09:38.780
Direct gap is when
the conduction

00:09:38.780 --> 00:09:40.970
and the valence bands,
so the upper band,

00:09:40.970 --> 00:09:44.540
we say conduction a
lower band is valence.

00:09:44.540 --> 00:09:47.540
So they are happening--

00:09:47.540 --> 00:09:51.260
the minimum and maximum is
at the same wave vector.

00:09:51.260 --> 00:09:55.520
While indirect is different.

00:09:55.520 --> 00:10:00.980
It could be here is the energy.

00:10:00.980 --> 00:10:03.650
Here is the wave vector.

00:10:03.650 --> 00:10:06.980
And they may happen at
different wave vectors.

00:10:09.670 --> 00:10:13.150
And this makes an
important distinction

00:10:13.150 --> 00:10:16.250
between gallium arsenide,
which is a direct gap

00:10:16.250 --> 00:10:20.980
semiconductor with a bandgap of
1.4 electron volt and silicon,

00:10:20.980 --> 00:10:23.350
which is an indirect
gap semiconductor

00:10:23.350 --> 00:10:25.330
with a bandgap of 1.1

00:10:25.330 --> 00:10:28.870
And you see most computer
chips are made of silicon.

00:10:28.870 --> 00:10:33.430
But most laser pointers,
made of gallium arsenide.

00:10:33.430 --> 00:10:38.290
This one, when you want to
make a light active device,

00:10:38.290 --> 00:10:42.760
direct gap is usually better,
because you need a light.

00:10:42.760 --> 00:10:45.590
Light itself do not
have much momentum.

00:10:45.590 --> 00:10:49.630
So here, k is the momentum.

00:10:49.630 --> 00:10:57.535
So h bar k is what we
call crystal momentum.

00:11:04.980 --> 00:11:10.110
It has some differences from the
real momentum of the electrons.

00:11:10.110 --> 00:11:13.590
But say, we're not getting
into that subtle difference.

00:11:13.590 --> 00:11:16.530
In typical way, you
can think is this

00:11:16.530 --> 00:11:19.680
is when I think about
momentum conservation of say,

00:11:19.680 --> 00:11:22.770
a light comes in, interact
with the electron.

00:11:22.770 --> 00:11:28.200
I use h bar k of the
electron in the crystal.

00:11:28.200 --> 00:11:33.360
Used h bar k for the photon.

00:11:33.360 --> 00:11:39.600
Now, the difference, of course,
remember k is 2 pi lambda.

00:11:39.600 --> 00:11:42.210
That's magnitude.

00:11:42.210 --> 00:11:49.530
So if you look at here in
crystal, my lattice constant,

00:11:49.530 --> 00:11:51.760
this is pi over a.

00:11:51.760 --> 00:11:56.010
That's angstrom in
lambda wavelengths.

00:11:56.010 --> 00:11:58.960
And photon lambda is a micron.

00:11:58.960 --> 00:12:04.300
So the photon momentum is
almost negligible, very small.

00:12:04.300 --> 00:12:08.850
And the electron
momentum, in this case,

00:12:08.850 --> 00:12:11.470
if they are different,
they're very large.

00:12:11.470 --> 00:12:14.280
That's why indirect gap
semiconductors do not absorb

00:12:14.280 --> 00:12:19.650
well, do not absorb light well.

00:12:19.650 --> 00:12:22.770
That's a big problem when
you make silicon solar cells.

00:12:22.770 --> 00:12:26.990
You need a lot of materials
to absorb the photon.

00:12:26.990 --> 00:12:30.230
So this is a direct
gap, indirect gap.

00:12:30.230 --> 00:12:35.960
And a little bit more
terminology for semiconductors.

00:12:35.960 --> 00:12:42.450
And if I do not put impurities,
do not put any, say,

00:12:42.450 --> 00:12:45.250
other atoms,
silicon, for example,

00:12:45.250 --> 00:12:48.360
at the absolute
zero temperature,

00:12:48.360 --> 00:12:51.480
the electrons will be here.

00:12:51.480 --> 00:12:53.520
That's the uppermost
energy level.

00:12:53.520 --> 00:12:58.830
That's the absolute zero
temperature, the Fermi level,

00:12:58.830 --> 00:13:02.300
where the Fermi level is.

00:13:02.300 --> 00:13:07.620
And now, at nonzero
temperature, some electrons

00:13:07.620 --> 00:13:10.050
gain enough thermal energy
because of the Boltzmann

00:13:10.050 --> 00:13:11.040
statistics.

00:13:11.040 --> 00:13:13.770
There are always electron
has distribution.

00:13:13.770 --> 00:13:18.300
And some electrons
move from here to here.

00:13:18.300 --> 00:13:21.410
And that leaves
behind empty states.

00:13:21.410 --> 00:13:23.660
And because empty
states, the electron

00:13:23.660 --> 00:13:27.530
here, free electron here,
are very happy because there

00:13:27.530 --> 00:13:31.600
are a lot of empty
states, many electron here

00:13:31.600 --> 00:13:34.090
are also happy because
now they finally have

00:13:34.090 --> 00:13:36.940
a little bit of room to wiggle.

00:13:36.940 --> 00:13:41.950
So the ones on the bottom,
rather than describing

00:13:41.950 --> 00:13:46.780
the motion of all
those electrons,

00:13:46.780 --> 00:13:50.230
fill the electron,
equivalent terminology

00:13:50.230 --> 00:13:54.180
is describe the motion
of those empty states.

00:13:54.180 --> 00:13:56.210
That's holes.

00:13:56.210 --> 00:13:58.640
And holes, when I
think about holes,

00:13:58.640 --> 00:14:01.210
energy is increasing
this way, because it's

00:14:01.210 --> 00:14:03.770
the negative of the electron.

00:14:03.770 --> 00:14:06.080
When I think about
electrons, the energy

00:14:06.080 --> 00:14:08.280
is increasing this way.

00:14:08.280 --> 00:14:11.030
So when I look at
this band diagram,

00:14:11.030 --> 00:14:12.650
when I think about
electron hole,

00:14:12.650 --> 00:14:17.540
I actually say this direction
is energy increasing for hole.

00:14:17.540 --> 00:14:19.980
That's the energy
increasing for electron.

00:14:19.980 --> 00:14:22.070
So we have electrons.

00:14:26.410 --> 00:14:27.190
We have holes.

00:14:29.890 --> 00:14:34.360
And here, where the electron
move is the conduction band.

00:14:41.970 --> 00:14:46.110
And whre the hole move, this
one is the valence band.

00:14:54.750 --> 00:15:00.660
And if it's a true intrinsic
semiconductor without doping,

00:15:00.660 --> 00:15:02.970
absolute zero is the insulator.

00:15:02.970 --> 00:15:05.850
Because it's for all
the charges here,

00:15:05.850 --> 00:15:07.870
and there's no moveable charge.

00:15:07.870 --> 00:15:12.460
So there are no empty states
for the electron to move.

00:15:12.460 --> 00:15:15.370
But see, normally, I
do not deal with-- when

00:15:15.370 --> 00:15:17.740
I use a semiconductor
device, I want

00:15:17.740 --> 00:15:22.720
to have a specific either
electron as a majority

00:15:22.720 --> 00:15:24.160
or hole as majority.

00:15:24.160 --> 00:15:25.480
So I dope it.

00:15:25.480 --> 00:15:30.920
So that's a intrinsic/extrinsic
semiconductor difference.

00:15:30.920 --> 00:15:38.950
So we have intrinsic, that's
when we do not dope it.

00:15:38.950 --> 00:15:41.530
And extrinsic, when we dope it.

00:15:45.200 --> 00:15:48.620
And when we dope, we can
dope with the n-type,

00:15:48.620 --> 00:15:50.660
when I put the phosphor.

00:15:50.660 --> 00:15:51.590
Or I dope p-type.

00:16:02.110 --> 00:16:06.840
So those are the semiconductors.

00:16:06.840 --> 00:16:11.220
And one more thing
I want to introduce.

00:16:11.220 --> 00:16:17.130
This is-- if I look
at the energy electron

00:16:17.130 --> 00:16:20.550
at the bottom or
hole at the top.

00:16:20.550 --> 00:16:24.920
And in fact, that's
the bottom of the hole,

00:16:24.920 --> 00:16:26.870
and when I do the--

00:16:26.870 --> 00:16:29.880
because this is at a
minimum/maximum point.

00:16:29.880 --> 00:16:32.850
So most of the time, the
charge in semiconductor,

00:16:32.850 --> 00:16:35.180
when you do not deal
with the heavily doped,

00:16:35.180 --> 00:16:39.350
they are only at the
bottom or top region here.

00:16:39.350 --> 00:16:41.780
And because of the
minimum/maximum,

00:16:41.780 --> 00:16:44.840
so I can use approximation.

00:16:44.840 --> 00:16:49.610
I can expand the potential
electrical energy

00:16:49.610 --> 00:16:52.080
around the minimum/maximum.

00:16:52.080 --> 00:16:58.280
So if I do an expansion,
typical expansion, So

00:16:58.280 --> 00:17:03.080
energy as a function of
k, and I'm writing 1D.

00:17:03.080 --> 00:17:06.770
You can say k in real crystal,
in each crystallographic

00:17:06.770 --> 00:17:09.079
direction, has a k.

00:17:09.079 --> 00:17:12.260
So I'm not writing
in vector form.

00:17:12.260 --> 00:17:14.359
But I can expand that.

00:17:14.359 --> 00:17:21.079
Let's say here, this is
EC conduction band edge.

00:17:21.079 --> 00:17:24.260
This is a k0.

00:17:24.260 --> 00:17:27.329
That's where the
minimum happens.

00:17:27.329 --> 00:17:33.290
So I'm going to expand
this as an EC k0.

00:17:33.290 --> 00:17:37.920
That's the first term
of the expansion.

00:17:37.920 --> 00:17:48.250
And the second term, dE
dk k0 and the k minus k0.

00:17:48.250 --> 00:17:58.155
And the last term is a dE dk
square and k minus k0 square.

00:18:02.470 --> 00:18:07.400
So this term, because
of maximum/minimum,

00:18:07.400 --> 00:18:13.920
is 0 as long as I do not
have a sharp turning.

00:18:13.920 --> 00:18:15.720
It's a linear profile.

00:18:15.720 --> 00:18:18.130
I cannot make that
approximation.

00:18:18.130 --> 00:18:21.480
So normally, this is 0.

00:18:21.480 --> 00:18:29.610
And this one, let me write
it into a form that's nicer.

00:18:29.610 --> 00:18:35.280
So I can write this h
square k minus k0 square

00:18:35.280 --> 00:18:41.790
and divide it by,
say, 2m, m as m star.

00:18:41.790 --> 00:18:44.460
So just a symbol.

00:18:44.460 --> 00:18:49.330
So this m star, because
I have h bar square,

00:18:49.330 --> 00:18:51.480
so I will have--
in the denominator,

00:18:51.480 --> 00:18:53.520
I will add h bar square.

00:18:53.520 --> 00:18:57.555
And the dE dk square.

00:19:00.510 --> 00:19:02.850
Do I?

00:19:02.850 --> 00:19:11.630
OK, right, so you
can say this m star

00:19:11.630 --> 00:19:18.530
is a second order to derivative
of this curve at the bottom.

00:19:18.530 --> 00:19:24.020
So if it's-- the
m star is large.

00:19:24.020 --> 00:19:26.980
How is that shape looking like?

00:19:26.980 --> 00:19:29.320
Flat.

00:19:29.320 --> 00:19:32.030
m bar is small.

00:19:32.030 --> 00:19:36.890
That shape, the parabola,
local parabola, is narrow.

00:19:36.890 --> 00:19:42.170
m star is large, the
local parabola is fat.

00:19:42.170 --> 00:19:45.750
So this mass is like--

00:19:45.750 --> 00:19:48.200
in fact, it has a unit of mass.

00:19:48.200 --> 00:19:54.020
If I write a free electron,
this hk is the momentum.

00:19:54.020 --> 00:19:56.285
Dive by 2m, that's
the kinetic energy.

00:19:58.910 --> 00:20:03.680
So by writing to this,
I introduce this m star

00:20:03.680 --> 00:20:05.060
as an effective mass.

00:20:15.420 --> 00:20:18.420
And using this
effective mass, this

00:20:18.420 --> 00:20:21.930
is hk, that's more like
a kinetic energy term.

00:20:21.930 --> 00:20:24.580
And it's a potential
energy term.

00:20:24.580 --> 00:20:28.410
So I can describe,
now, the electron

00:20:28.410 --> 00:20:30.570
in this small
band, the transport

00:20:30.570 --> 00:20:36.030
as essentially inside the
crystal as a free electron

00:20:36.030 --> 00:20:39.730
but with a different
mass, m star.

00:20:39.730 --> 00:20:41.860
And in fact, and
now you can say,

00:20:41.860 --> 00:20:45.740
because this k in a real
crystal in different direction

00:20:45.740 --> 00:20:49.290
is different, so the mass
in different direction

00:20:49.290 --> 00:20:50.510
is different.

00:20:50.510 --> 00:20:53.490
So in fact, many
times, this m star

00:20:53.490 --> 00:20:59.670
is written as a tensor, rather
than just a single value there.

00:20:59.670 --> 00:21:03.270
And that depends on
different materials.

00:21:03.270 --> 00:21:09.020
So this is both a
overview of last lecture

00:21:09.020 --> 00:21:14.390
and also some expansion in
terms of the effect of mass.

00:21:14.390 --> 00:21:19.730
And where I'm going today
is continue this 3D picture.

00:21:19.730 --> 00:21:23.330
Now we have 1D picture,
we're going 3D.

00:21:23.330 --> 00:21:28.010
So in the 3D, I want to
first talk about the crystal

00:21:28.010 --> 00:21:29.615
structure to get more familiar.

00:21:40.840 --> 00:21:45.400
Then, I will talk about what are
the bonding between the atoms

00:21:45.400 --> 00:21:47.530
that hold the crystal together.

00:21:47.530 --> 00:21:53.020
And finally, we'll use a
mathematical terminology

00:21:53.020 --> 00:21:55.450
is how we can--

00:21:55.450 --> 00:21:58.840
it's a periodic
function in 3D and what

00:21:58.840 --> 00:22:01.660
are the corresponding
Fourier transform.

00:22:01.660 --> 00:22:05.382
We did a one-dimensional,
we say this k essentially

00:22:05.382 --> 00:22:07.520
are the Fourier transform.

00:22:07.520 --> 00:22:10.160
So this is where
we're going today.

00:22:10.160 --> 00:22:13.630
Let's look at the
crystal structures first.

00:22:13.630 --> 00:22:16.180
And at the end of
the lecture, I'll

00:22:16.180 --> 00:22:19.780
show the best is by looking
at it more visually.

00:22:19.780 --> 00:22:20.710
I can't draw that way.

00:22:20.710 --> 00:22:24.520
So I'll use a 2D as an example.

00:22:24.520 --> 00:22:29.350
But at the end, we'll
look at the 3D online.

00:22:29.350 --> 00:22:34.560
So crystals can be
described-- the most important

00:22:34.560 --> 00:22:37.590
characteristic of crystal
is the periodicity, periodic

00:22:37.590 --> 00:22:40.410
arrangement of atoms.

00:22:40.410 --> 00:22:44.340
And they have
different atoms putting

00:22:44.340 --> 00:22:45.610
into the same structure.

00:22:45.610 --> 00:22:50.830
So a crystal we can
describe as a lattice,

00:22:50.830 --> 00:22:57.120
which is a mathematical
concept plus basis.

00:22:57.120 --> 00:23:03.090
So lattice is a periodic
arrangement of points in space.

00:23:03.090 --> 00:23:05.220
And it's mathematical concept.

00:23:05.220 --> 00:23:08.610
Now devoid of any
real materials.

00:23:08.610 --> 00:23:12.420
And by doing that,
you can actually

00:23:12.420 --> 00:23:18.010
classify different structures
of these periodic points.

00:23:18.010 --> 00:23:22.680
So let's take a look at a
a simple 2D, like I said.

00:23:31.150 --> 00:23:33.190
And 2D, but there
is a center point.

00:23:36.600 --> 00:23:39.240
2D structure,
periodic arrangement.

00:23:39.240 --> 00:23:43.620
And we introduce a
few basic terminology.

00:23:43.620 --> 00:23:46.245
One is primitive lattice vector.

00:24:01.640 --> 00:24:07.040
So in 3D, will be a1, a2, a3.

00:24:07.040 --> 00:24:10.870
In 2D, of course,
we only have two.

00:24:10.870 --> 00:24:13.060
And what is a primitive
lattice vector?

00:24:13.060 --> 00:24:17.230
It's a set of vector that I
can use the linear combination

00:24:17.230 --> 00:24:20.380
to reach all other
points by integers.

00:24:20.380 --> 00:24:24.910
So I can use this
primitive lattice vector

00:24:24.910 --> 00:24:35.350
to construct a translation
vector n1 a1 plus n2 a2 plus n3

00:24:35.350 --> 00:24:42.490
a3 while n1, n2,
n3 are integers.

00:24:49.650 --> 00:24:54.450
OK, so this is the
primitive lattice vector.

00:24:54.450 --> 00:24:57.930
And the problem is
if I look at this 2D,

00:24:57.930 --> 00:25:01.170
I have a different ways to
choose the primitive lattice

00:25:01.170 --> 00:25:03.270
vector.

00:25:03.270 --> 00:25:05.340
Here is one way.

00:25:05.340 --> 00:25:10.310
If I have this one and this one.

00:25:10.310 --> 00:25:13.620
If you look at this,
I can use these two

00:25:13.620 --> 00:25:15.485
and the linear
combination of this two

00:25:15.485 --> 00:25:18.090
to reach any other points.

00:25:18.090 --> 00:25:23.100
So this is, let's say,
a1 and this is a2.

00:25:23.100 --> 00:25:26.370
And of course, you could
also choose a different one.

00:25:26.370 --> 00:25:29.460
You can choose this
one and this one.

00:25:32.390 --> 00:25:35.990
So one of the challenge is
how to narrow down our choice,

00:25:35.990 --> 00:25:40.400
how to uniquely define this
choice of primitive lattice

00:25:40.400 --> 00:25:42.020
vectors.

00:25:42.020 --> 00:25:46.430
The other is a
conventional unit cell.

00:25:46.430 --> 00:25:49.430
So primitive lattice
vector, those vectors

00:25:49.430 --> 00:25:55.620
will form a unit
area or unit volume.

00:25:55.620 --> 00:25:56.120
so?

00:25:56.120 --> 00:25:58.550
In 2D is by this unit area.

00:25:58.550 --> 00:26:02.990
If I choose this,
this is my unit area.

00:26:02.990 --> 00:26:07.280
And I just repeat it, I
will pile up the 2D space

00:26:07.280 --> 00:26:10.170
without any voids.

00:26:10.170 --> 00:26:14.670
And the similarly, in 3D, I
choose my primitive-- this unit

00:26:14.670 --> 00:26:16.290
vector from a volume.

00:26:16.290 --> 00:26:18.640
That's a primitive unit cell.

00:26:18.640 --> 00:26:22.660
So this will form a
primitive unit cell.

00:26:32.280 --> 00:26:38.640
And so this is the one
choice of the vectors.

00:26:38.640 --> 00:26:41.340
And the other choice
is conventional.

00:26:41.340 --> 00:26:49.980
You can say unit cell.

00:26:57.830 --> 00:27:00.710
And say if you look
at this, it probably

00:27:00.710 --> 00:27:07.710
is much easier if you choose
this as your unit cell.

00:27:07.710 --> 00:27:10.110
It looks more regular.

00:27:10.110 --> 00:27:14.220
And if you look at the
difference of a primitive unit

00:27:14.220 --> 00:27:16.200
cell and the
conventional unit cell.

00:27:16.200 --> 00:27:19.590
A primitive unit cell, each
of the cells has the only one

00:27:19.590 --> 00:27:23.530
lattice point, equivalently.

00:27:23.530 --> 00:27:26.440
This is because although
this primitive unit

00:27:26.440 --> 00:27:29.620
cell has four lattice points,
each of this lattice ts

00:27:29.620 --> 00:27:32.030
is shared by four unit cells.

00:27:32.030 --> 00:27:35.470
So each of the
lattice point is 1/4.

00:27:35.470 --> 00:27:39.840
You add up, it's one
point, one lattice point

00:27:39.840 --> 00:27:42.240
in one primitive unit cell.

00:27:42.240 --> 00:27:44.340
But if I take this
conventional one,

00:27:44.340 --> 00:27:48.570
you can say this one is shared
by four, So 1/4, 1/4, that's--

00:27:48.570 --> 00:27:53.070
in my current drawing, there are
two points, two lattice points

00:27:53.070 --> 00:27:54.810
in one conventional unit cell.

00:27:58.810 --> 00:28:04.750
And they are-- so like I said,
one of the challenge is even

00:28:04.750 --> 00:28:08.800
if I take a primitive unit cell,
each of the cell is one lattice

00:28:08.800 --> 00:28:12.320
point, I do not
have a unique way--

00:28:12.320 --> 00:28:15.320
the way I have many choices.

00:28:15.320 --> 00:28:21.400
So there is a cumbersome,
but the only way to do it,

00:28:21.400 --> 00:28:27.170
to define a primitive unit cell.

00:28:27.170 --> 00:28:32.060
And this is a
Wigner-Seitz construction.

00:28:32.060 --> 00:28:34.580
So let me extend
this drawing here.

00:28:38.180 --> 00:28:40.895
I will extend to this
side to make it clear.

00:28:45.360 --> 00:28:51.240
So one way to construct this
is to take any of these lattice

00:28:51.240 --> 00:28:56.310
point, here is a
lattice point, I

00:28:56.310 --> 00:29:00.470
collect all the
neighboring lattice points.

00:29:00.470 --> 00:29:02.675
So I connect with all
the neighboring points.

00:29:10.740 --> 00:29:15.810
And then I take the
bisect of those points

00:29:15.810 --> 00:29:17.410
here, of these lines.

00:29:17.410 --> 00:29:19.350
So this is the bisect.

00:29:19.350 --> 00:29:22.050
And here is bisect.

00:29:22.050 --> 00:29:23.280
And here is a bisect.

00:29:26.250 --> 00:29:30.350
And you have another point here.

00:29:30.350 --> 00:29:34.030
And it turns out this one
is not a regular cell.

00:29:34.030 --> 00:29:39.060
It doesn't look very
good, but it's a unique.

00:29:39.060 --> 00:29:42.060
Because any crystal structure,
I can take lattice point.

00:29:42.060 --> 00:29:44.100
I connect all the
neighboring points.

00:29:44.100 --> 00:29:45.390
I take the bisects.

00:29:45.390 --> 00:29:50.190
And the minimum volume in
this case of area, the form

00:29:50.190 --> 00:29:53.400
is a primitive unit cell.

00:29:53.400 --> 00:29:57.750
And I can use that to stack
together and form-- and fill

00:29:57.750 --> 00:29:59.535
the whole space
without the voids.

00:30:02.210 --> 00:30:04.940
So it's not the
easiest construction,

00:30:04.940 --> 00:30:08.030
but it's a unique construction.

00:30:08.030 --> 00:30:14.630
And this is for the
Wigner-Seitz construction.

00:30:29.500 --> 00:30:31.060
Wigner-Seitz unit cell.

00:30:31.060 --> 00:30:33.640
Wigner-Seitz cell, because
it's always-- this construction

00:30:33.640 --> 00:30:36.130
always give you unit cell.

00:30:36.130 --> 00:30:41.470
OK, so this unit
cell gives you--

00:30:41.470 --> 00:30:44.980
now we have the
primitive lattice vector,

00:30:44.980 --> 00:30:47.440
the primitive unit cell.

00:30:47.440 --> 00:30:52.720
And as I said, this lattice
is a mathematical concept.

00:30:52.720 --> 00:30:55.750
So there are different--
required different symmetry.

00:30:55.750 --> 00:31:13.140
The most-- in 3D, for example,
the easiest is a triclinic unit

00:31:13.140 --> 00:31:17.450
cell that I can
stack and form the--

00:31:17.450 --> 00:31:20.670
form the whole space,
fill the whole space.

00:31:20.670 --> 00:31:22.400
So there is no symmetry.

00:31:22.400 --> 00:31:25.580
The angle, there's
no line of degree.

00:31:25.580 --> 00:31:29.750
And the lattice constant
between here-- the unit

00:31:29.750 --> 00:31:32.190
cell of the lattice comes in
each direction are different.

00:31:32.190 --> 00:31:35.200
So this is a general one.

00:31:35.200 --> 00:31:37.330
But then there are
different symmetry.

00:31:37.330 --> 00:31:40.390
If you add the
symmetries, it turns out

00:31:40.390 --> 00:31:50.270
there are only a total of 14
types of the crystal lattice.

00:32:01.770 --> 00:32:07.320
And I will defer this later
on to show you on computer.

00:32:07.320 --> 00:32:16.660
And these are the 14 types,
also called the Bravais lattice.

00:32:21.580 --> 00:32:25.630
So well look at this
in computer later on.

00:32:25.630 --> 00:32:30.850
And one more convention
of this lattice,

00:32:30.850 --> 00:32:34.890
the mathematical description, is
the different crystallographic

00:32:34.890 --> 00:32:36.770
plans.

00:32:36.770 --> 00:32:45.080
So if I take a cubic
structure, a unit seal that's

00:32:45.080 --> 00:32:50.480
made of cube, my
question is how I

00:32:50.480 --> 00:32:56.580
would denote different faces,
different crystallographic

00:32:56.580 --> 00:32:57.390
directions.

00:33:02.990 --> 00:33:06.770
And the different
crystallographic planes.

00:33:06.770 --> 00:33:10.040
And the convention
that's usually

00:33:10.040 --> 00:33:15.380
used in define the
crystallographic planes,

00:33:15.380 --> 00:33:17.360
let's say this is 3D.

00:33:17.360 --> 00:33:22.640
I have a1, a2, a3.

00:33:26.200 --> 00:33:29.740
And I have any crystallographic
plane, for example,

00:33:29.740 --> 00:33:31.340
from here, going here.

00:33:31.340 --> 00:33:32.680
This is the middle point.

00:33:32.680 --> 00:33:35.300
And I could have this point.

00:33:35.300 --> 00:33:42.560
So this form a crystallographic
plan, any of those.

00:33:42.560 --> 00:33:48.390
So if I have a plan that
intersect within the unit cell,

00:33:48.390 --> 00:33:51.200
let's say at one point--

00:33:51.200 --> 00:33:55.130
in a1 direction, I have an
intersection point, a2, a3.

00:33:55.130 --> 00:33:59.040
Let me give you an arbitrary
number as an example.

00:33:59.040 --> 00:34:03.930
So this is 0.8 in terms of
the total length of unit cell.

00:34:03.930 --> 00:34:06.800
And here is the 0.4.

00:34:06.800 --> 00:34:10.429
And here is 0.6.

00:34:10.429 --> 00:34:16.909
This is a plane within
that cube there.

00:34:16.909 --> 00:34:23.449
And the way I label these planes
is this Miller index method.

00:34:30.320 --> 00:34:33.560
And you take a
first stab, you take

00:34:33.560 --> 00:34:37.090
a reciprocal of those numbers.

00:34:37.090 --> 00:34:48.489
The reciprocal is 0.8,
0.4, and then say 0.6.

00:34:48.489 --> 00:34:51.730
You take, say, inverse
of those intersects

00:34:51.730 --> 00:34:55.690
and then you'll reduce
these two integers.

00:34:55.690 --> 00:35:03.990
You multiply by
the common factors.

00:35:03.990 --> 00:35:09.900
So if we multiply, reduce this
to integer, I multiply by 2.4.

00:35:09.900 --> 00:35:13.830
And I get a 3, 6, 4.

00:35:13.830 --> 00:35:18.630
So that's the crystallographic
plan that we just draw.

00:35:18.630 --> 00:35:19.920
This is the convention.

00:35:22.800 --> 00:35:24.578
And you say that's
pretty cumbersome.

00:35:24.578 --> 00:35:25.620
Why you take the inverse?

00:35:29.770 --> 00:35:34.330
That's one way to think about
this is if I look at this cube

00:35:34.330 --> 00:35:39.680
again, for example,
this surface here,

00:35:39.680 --> 00:35:45.390
and if I do not take an
inverse, you see my intersect.

00:35:45.390 --> 00:35:47.120
So this is a1.

00:35:47.120 --> 00:35:49.580
This is a2.

00:35:49.580 --> 00:35:51.290
And this is a3.

00:35:51.290 --> 00:35:56.420
And if I look at this surface
here, that intersect here is 1.

00:35:56.420 --> 00:35:58.776
And the other two
direction is infinite.

00:36:01.640 --> 00:36:04.670
So this surface, there
will be intersect is

00:36:04.670 --> 00:36:06.830
1, infinite, infinite.

00:36:10.190 --> 00:36:12.860
And I guess people
don't like the infinite,

00:36:12.860 --> 00:36:16.160
so take an inverse.

00:36:16.160 --> 00:36:20.230
And that becomes 1, 0, 0 plane.

00:36:24.480 --> 00:36:28.590
So that's just the
convention of denoting

00:36:28.590 --> 00:36:30.780
specific
crystallographic planes.

00:36:34.320 --> 00:36:38.270
OK, now, here is so
far is mathematics.

00:36:38.270 --> 00:36:40.630
So we say the lattice
point is abstract.

00:36:40.630 --> 00:36:42.330
There's no mass.

00:36:42.330 --> 00:36:43.500
Just periodic array.

00:36:43.500 --> 00:36:46.950
And there are 14 types.

00:36:46.950 --> 00:36:50.100
But we have so many
variations in real world

00:36:50.100 --> 00:36:55.120
different crystals,
different materials.

00:36:55.120 --> 00:36:59.770
So structurally,
among the 14 types,

00:36:59.770 --> 00:37:03.810
the difference is really
at each lattice point,

00:37:03.810 --> 00:37:06.810
what do you put there?

00:37:06.810 --> 00:37:10.710
What you put there are each
lattice point is a basis.

00:37:16.270 --> 00:37:20.770
And you can put a single
atom at each lattice point.

00:37:20.770 --> 00:37:23.490
You can put two atoms
at each lattice point.

00:37:23.490 --> 00:37:28.090
Or put the molecule
at each lattice point.

00:37:28.090 --> 00:37:31.840
So salt is common.

00:37:31.840 --> 00:37:35.320
Salt is among the
14 types later on,

00:37:35.320 --> 00:37:39.310
is face centered cubic, with
a cubic structure with one

00:37:39.310 --> 00:37:41.110
atom at the face.

00:37:41.110 --> 00:37:44.170
Let's say at each of
those lattice point,

00:37:44.170 --> 00:37:50.640
you actually put a
sodium and chloride atom.

00:37:50.640 --> 00:37:52.800
So OK, each lattice
point doesn't mean

00:37:52.800 --> 00:37:55.480
they occupy the same space.

00:37:55.480 --> 00:37:58.920
It's just periodically, you
can say, OK, this is a sodium.

00:37:58.920 --> 00:38:02.310
And another direction,
you have chloride.

00:38:02.310 --> 00:38:06.760
You just repeat that
for each lattice point.

00:38:06.760 --> 00:38:09.040
This will become
more clear later on

00:38:09.040 --> 00:38:12.820
as we look at the computer.

00:38:12.820 --> 00:38:15.620
I can't draw very well,
so I will rely on that.

00:38:15.620 --> 00:38:21.160
And similarly, silicon crystal.

00:38:21.160 --> 00:38:28.430
And silicon crystal, it turns
out, at each lattice point,

00:38:28.430 --> 00:38:33.350
you put two silicon,
a silicon and silicon.

00:38:36.420 --> 00:38:40.680
So again, that's a--

00:38:40.680 --> 00:38:45.480
it's the same everywhere
is a silicon atom.

00:38:45.480 --> 00:38:48.270
But if you look at
the crystal itself,

00:38:48.270 --> 00:38:52.110
or you actually put two silicon
atoms in specific direction

00:38:52.110 --> 00:38:56.180
with specific angle that
from the silicon crystal.

00:38:56.180 --> 00:39:02.840
So those are the basis for
what we call lattice plus basis

00:39:02.840 --> 00:39:04.770
together to form the crystal.

00:39:07.670 --> 00:39:13.520
OK, let's hold that we'll
come back and look at it.

